 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to legalize, validate, ratify and confirm the actions of the Panama\ncentral school district notwithstanding the failure to timely file final\nbuilding cost reports with the education department",
    "basePrintNo": "S7034",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7034-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 4,
       "fullName": "Phil Boyle",
       "imgName": "433_phil_boyle.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 433,
       "sessionMemberId": 1289,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "BOYLE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7034\n\nSPONSOR: BORRELLO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to legalize, validate, ratify and confirm the actions of the\nPanama central school district notwithstanding the failure to timely\nfile final building cost reports with the education department\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nLegalizes, validates, ratifies and confirms the actions of the Panama\ncentral school district regardless of the failure of such district to\ntimely file final building cost reports.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Allows the Education Department to consider the final cost\nreport for Panama Central School as legalized, validated, ratified and\nconfirmed for approved building projects completed prior to December 31,\n2012 recognizing that the failure to submit the report in a timely\nmanner was an inadvertent administrative or ministerial oversight.\n\nSection 2. Provides that the Commissioner of the State Education Depart-\nment shall not recover from the Panama Central School District any\npenalty arising from the late filing of a final cost report pursuant to\nsection 31 of part A of chapter 57 of the laws of 2012, provided that\nany amounts already so recovered shall be deemed a payment for money due\nfor prior years pursuant to paragraph c of subdivision 5 of section 3604\nof the education law and shall be paid to the Panama Central School\nDistrict pursuant to such provision.\n\nSection 3. Establishes that the act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Panama Central School District was assessed a $4.9 million penalty\nas a result of a late final cost report from a 2005 capital project.  In\n2005, the Panama Central School District completed a small maintenance\nproject that was closed out. In 2009, Panama Central School District\nverified that this project was complete as shown on the state website.\nPanama then started a new project in 2008.\n\nIn 2012, Panama Central School reviewed the state's website to find that\nthey had an \"open\" project with a substantial completion certificate\nfiled on August 28, 2002 and December 12, 2005. This information was not\npreviously made available to Panama Central School when they were clos-\ning out their 2005 building project. This meant that Panama Central\nSchool neglected to file a Final Cost Report for a building project with\nthe Office of Facility Planning of the State Education Department (SED)\nwithin the established time frame.\n\nIn the 2013-14 State Budget, a plan was negotiated that allowed for a\n10-year repayment plan to help the school manage what remained a tremen-\ndous penalty for a small rural school district. Also provided in the\n2013-14 State Budget was a $500,000 Supplemental Valuation Impact Grant:\nthrough the Education Department/Aid to Localities budget bill, which\nwas provided for again in the 2014-15 State Budget, the 2015-16 State\nBudget, the 2016-17 State Budget, the 2017-18 State Budget, and the\n2018-19 State Budget. This bill would for give the remaining amount,\n$1.9 million, of the assessed penalty.  Without full forgiveness of this\npenalty, the students who attend Panama Central School and the\nDistrict's taxpayers will be left holding the bag for administrative\noversights made over a decade ago.\n\nWith our small, rural schools already facing the heavy burdens of limit-\ned state aid, tax revenues and loss of enrollment and tax base, Panama\nCentral School faces particularly significant cash flow problems that\nthreaten its very operations if not addressed.\n\nPanama and Clymer Central School Districts previously laid plans to\nmerge but the community had voiced tremendous concern over inheriting\nPanama's final cost report penalties, leading to the merger being voted\ndown in November 2017. As such, locally, many individuals continue to\nview this overwhelming obstacle as a key factor in the merger vote's\nfailure.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: S.6365/A.5442 Vetoed Memo 124\n2018: S.7383/A.9730 Vetoed Memo 357\n2017: S.6779/A.8302-A Vetoed Memo 170\n2015,2016: S.7416/A.100 45 Passed Senate\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\n$1.9 million in remaining final cost report penalty\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
